---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: AI and tooling
description: Connect an agent to Aihio through its MCP server, prompt fragment, linter, and schema.
---
<section class="docs-page-intro">
  <p class="docs-page-intro__meta">Start here</p>
  <h1>Built to be written by agents</h1>
  <p>
    Every component has a machine-readable contract, and the same build turns it into a prompt fragment, a linter
    that suggests fixes, an MCP server, types, and this site. They are generated from one schema, so they cannot
    disagree: if the linter accepts markup, the prompt taught it and the docs show it.
  </p>
</section>

<nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#connect">Connect an agent</a></li>
    <li><a href="#tools">MCP tools</a></li>
    <li><a href="#loop">The lint loop</a></li>
    <li><a href="#files">Files to read</a></li>
    <li><a href="#runtime">In the browser</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>

<section id="connect">
  <div class="docs-section-heading">
    <div>
      <h2>Connect an agent</h2>
      <p>
        <code>aihio-mcp</code> is a local stdio server that ships in the package. With <code>@luntta/aihio</code> installed in
        the project, add it to the client that writes your UI.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-examples">
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">Claude Code</h3>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>claude mcp add aihio -- npx aihio-mcp</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">Any client with a JSON config</h3>
      <p class="docs-example__description">Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, and other MCP clients take the same entry.</p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "aihio": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["aihio-mcp"]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">Without MCP</h3>
      <p class="docs-example__description">
        Put the prompt fragment in the system prompt or the project's instructions, and lint what comes back.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>import prompt from '@luntta/aihio/prompt';
import { lintMarkup } from '@luntta/aihio/lint';</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="tools">
  <div class="docs-section-heading">
    <div>
      <h2>MCP tools</h2>
      <p>Read from the server itself. It also serves the prompt fragment as the prompt <code>{{ docs.ai.prompt.name }}</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="docs-api">
    {% for tool in docs.ai.tools %}
      <div class="docs-api__row">
        <dt><code>{{ tool.name }}</code></dt>
        {% if tool.parameters.length %}
          <dd class="docs-api__type">
            {% for parameter in tool.parameters %}<code>{{ parameter.name }}{% if not parameter.required %}?{% endif %}</code>{% endfor %}
          </dd>
        {% endif %}
        <dd class="docs-api__description">{{ tool.description | prose | safe }}</dd>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>

  <div class="docs-example docs-ai-sample">
    <h3 class="docs-example__title">What <code>find</code> returns</h3>
    <p class="docs-example__description">Run at build time, for <code>"{{ docs.ai.find.query }}"</code>. Scores are trimmed from the full entries.</p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>{{ docs.ai.find.result | escape }}</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="loop">
  <div class="docs-section-heading">
    <div>
      <h2>The lint loop</h2>
      <p>
        Lint every snippet before returning it. An issue with a <code>suggestion</code> is fixed by applying the
        suggestion, so a model needs one round trip, not a reading of the docs. This is the linter's real output.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-examples">
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">1. Markup in the shape of other design systems</h3>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{{ docs.ai.lint.markup | highlightHtml | safe }}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">2. <code>lintMarkup()</code> returns</h3>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{{ docs.ai.lint.result | escape }}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-example">
      <h3 class="docs-example__title">3. Both suggestions applied</h3>
      <p class="docs-example__description">{{ docs.ai.lint.fixedIssues }} issues. The link is a real link, drawn as the button.</p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{{ docs.ai.lint.fixed | highlightHtml | safe }}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="files">
  <div class="docs-section-heading">
    <div>
      <h2>Files to read</h2>
      <p>
        Everything ships in the package. This site also serves <a class="docs-inline-link" href="{{ '/llms.txt' | docsUrl(page.url) }}">llms.txt</a>,
        <a class="docs-inline-link" href="{{ '/llms-full.txt' | docsUrl(page.url) }}">llms-full.txt</a>, and every
        component page as markdown (<a class="docs-inline-link" href="{{ '/components/button/index.md' | docsUrl(page.url) }}">components/button/index.md</a>),
        which the "Copy for agent" button on each component page copies.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="docs-api">
    {% for artifact in docs.ai.artifacts %}
      <div class="docs-api__row">
        <dt><a class="docs-code-link" href="{{ ('/' + artifact.path) | docsUrl(page.url) }}"><code>{{ artifact.path }}</code></a></dt>
        <dd class="docs-api__type">
          <span class="docs-api__kind">import</span><code>{{ artifact.exportName }}</code>
          <span class="docs-api__default">{{ artifact.size }}</span>
        </dd>
        <dd class="docs-api__description">{{ artifact.what }}</dd>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="runtime">
  <div class="docs-section-heading">
    <div>
      <h2>In the browser</h2>
      <p>The rules an agent reads are the ones the page enforces while you build.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="docs-api">
    <div class="docs-api__row">
      <dt><code>aihio/dev</code></dt>
      <dd class="docs-api__description">
        The same components, with console warnings for invalid attribute values and broken accessibility
        obligations, checked by the same rules as the linter. The production bundle carries none of it.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-api__row">
      <dt><code>Aihio.describe(tag)</code></dt>
      <dd class="docs-api__description">A component's schema at runtime, for tools that inspect a live page.</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-api__row">
      <dt><code>data-aihio-intent</code></dt>
      <dd class="docs-api__description">
        Says what an element is for, from the <a class="docs-inline-link" href="{{ '/intents/' | docsUrl(page.url) }}">intent vocabulary</a>.
        Optional, but the linter rejects an annotation the component's schema does not back.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-api__row">
      <dt><code>dist/aihio.d.ts</code></dt>
      <dd class="docs-api__description">Types generated from the schema: attribute unions, intents, and JSX intrinsic elements.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>
